/// \defgroup grp_w1021_special_power_factories_more More special power modules and their factories
/// \ingroup grp_objects_modules
/// \brief Fifteen more of BFME 2's special-power module classes, from
///        ActivateModuleSpecialPower to UntamedAllegianceSpecialPower, and
///        the factory functions the module factory calls to build their
///        module data from INI and their modules for each object.
///
/// This group continues \ref grp_w1020_special_power_module_factories,
/// which describes the shared mechanism: ModuleFactory::init registers each
/// module name with an instance factory, a data factory, an interface mask
/// and a module type; ModuleFactory::newModuleDataFromINI calls the data
/// factory while INI loads, and ModuleFactory::newModule calls the
/// instance factory when an object is built. Read that page first; this
/// one covers what differs for the classes here.
///
/// \par What is documented here
/// - Instance factories (\c friend_newModuleInstance) of twelve module
///   classes: CombineHordeSpecialPower, DeflectSpecialPower,
///   ManTheWallsSpecialPower, PlayerUpgradeSpecialPower,
///   RepairSpecialPower, ScavengerSpecialPower,
///   SiegeDeployHordeSpecialPower, SiegeDeploySpecialPower,
///   SplitHordeSpecialPower, StopSpecialPower, StoreObjectsSpecialPower
///   and UntamedAllegianceSpecialPower.
/// - Data factories (\c friend_newModuleData) of twelve module data
///   classes, including SpecialPowerModuleData itself and the data of
///   ActivateModuleSpecialPower, HordeDispatchSpecialPower and
///   OCLSpecialPower, whose instance factories are not covered here.
/// - The data factories of CombineHordeSpecialPower,
///   ScavengerSpecialPower and StoreObjectsSpecialPower are not covered
///   here, but the class pages list the fields their blocks accept.
///
/// \par Registrations
/// Every module name below is registered as a logic (behavior) module, so
/// it appears in an `Object` block as `Behavior = <name> ModuleTag_...`
/// (\ref page_modding_modules). The interface masks in
/// `reverse/module_factory_registrations.csv`, read with Zero Hour's bit
/// values, sort the classes into three kinds:
/// - **Special power only**: CombineHordeSpecialPower,
///   HordeDispatchSpecialPower, ManTheWallsSpecialPower, OCLSpecialPower,
///   PlayerUpgradeSpecialPower, RepairSpecialPower, ScavengerSpecialPower,
///   SplitHordeSpecialPower, StopSpecialPower and
///   UntamedAllegianceSpecialPower, as well as the plain
///   \c SpecialPowerModule and \c SpecialPowerTimerRefreshSpecialPower
///   names.
/// - **Special power and update**: SiegeDeploySpecialPower and
///   SiegeDeployHordeSpecialPower. DeflectSpecialPower adds the collide
///   interface to these two.
/// - **Update only**: ActivateModuleSpecialPower and
///   StoreObjectsSpecialPower. Like the special-ability update modules on
///   \ref grp_w1020_special_power_module_factories, their data starts from
///   SpecialAbilityUpdateModuleData::buildFieldParse, not from the
///   special-power table.
///
/// \par Shared factories
/// The registrations do not pair every name with a factory of its own:
/// - \c SpecialPowerModule and \c SpecialPowerTimerRefreshSpecialPower
///   both register SpecialPowerModuleData::friend_newModuleData, so both
///   accept exactly the shared special-power fields.
/// - \c ManTheWallsSpecialPower and \c SplitHordeSpecialPower both
///   register the data factory named
///   ManTheWallsSpecialPowerModuleData::friend_newModuleData;
///   ModuleFactory::init registers ManTheWallsSpecialPower first.
/// - The data factories of UntamedAllegianceSpecialPower,
///   ManTheWallsSpecialPower, RepairSpecialPower and
///   HordeDispatchSpecialPower allocate the same size and run the same
///   constructor: the executable keeps one copy of their identical
///   constructor, so objects of the four data types cannot be told apart
///   by type at run time. Each module line still gets its own data
///   object, and each factory still passes its own parse procedure, but
///   none of those procedures adds a field to
///   SpecialPowerModuleData::buildFieldParse (three add an empty table,
///   ManTheWalls's jumps straight to it).
///
/// \par Field tables
/// Each class page lists the fields read from the retail executable's own
/// field tables, which the \c buildFieldParse procedures pass to the INI
/// reader. Two shared tables come first:
/// - **The special-power table** (SpecialPowerModuleData::buildFieldParse)
///   has \c SpecialPowerTemplate, \c UpdateModuleStartsAttack,
///   \c StartsPaused, \c InitiateSound, \c ReEnableAntiCategory,
///   \c AntiCategory, \c AntiFX, \c AttributeModifier,
///   \c AttributeModifierRange, \c AttributeModifierAffectsSelf,
///   \c AttributeModifierAffects, \c AttributeModifierFX,
///   \c AttributeModifierWeatherBased, \c WeatherDuration,
///   \c RequirementsFilterMPSkirmish, \c RequirementsFilterStrategic,
///   \c TargetEnemy, \c TargetAllSides, \c InitiateFX, \c TriggerFX,
///   \c SetModelCondition, \c SetModelConditionTime, \c GiveLevels,
///   \c DisableDuringAnimDuration, \c IdleWhenStartingPower,
///   \c AffectGood, \c AffectEvil, \c AffectAllies, \c AvailableAtStart,
///   \c ChangeWeather, \c AdjustVictim, \c OnTriggerRechargeSpecialPower,
///   \c BurnDecayModifier, \c UseDistanceFromCommandCenter and
///   \c DistanceFromCommandCenter (the whole table).
/// - **A short three-field table** with \c SpecialPowerTemplate,
///   \c InitiateSound and \c StartsPaused. DeflectSpecialPower,
///   SiegeDeploySpecialPower, SiegeDeployHordeSpecialPower and
///   WeaponModeSpecialPowerUpdate start from it instead of the
///   special-power table. This page does not identify the class that owns
///   it.
///
/// \par Origins
/// Of the module classes here, only OCLSpecialPower is in Zero Hour (as is
/// SpecialPowerModuleData, the shared base data). Open-BFME-1's \c game/
/// tree has sources for the others except ActivateModuleSpecialPower,
/// HordeDispatchSpecialPower, StoreObjectsSpecialPower and
/// UntamedAllegianceSpecialPower, which it does not mention.
///
/// \par Modding
/// - Data-driven: which objects carry these modules, their module tags,
///   and the fields on the class pages. The special power each module
///   answers to is a separate \c SpecialPower definition, named by the
///   module's \c SpecialPowerTemplate field
///   (\ref page_core_frameworks_upgrades).
/// - Hard-coded: the module names, which data factory each name uses, and
///   what each module does with its fields. A name whose parse procedure
///   adds no fields of its own (ManTheWalls, SplitHorde, Repair,
///   HordeDispatch and UntamedAllegiance, plus the \c SpecialPowerModule
///   and \c SpecialPowerTimerRefreshSpecialPower names) accepts only the
///   special-power table; Deflect accepts only the short three-field
///   table.
/// - The multiplayer, save and tag rules on
///   \ref grp_w1020_special_power_module_factories apply unchanged: these
///   are game-logic modules, so every machine must load the same values.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// \c LowerDelay and \c RaiseDelay (SiegeDeploySpecialPower) and the
/// shared \c WeatherDuration go through INI::parseDurationUnsignedInt, so
/// they are stored as logic frames computed at load time; the remastering
/// note on \ref grp_w1020_special_power_module_factories explains what
/// that means for a change of logic rate.
///
/// \see ModuleFactory::newModuleDataFromINI, ModuleFactory::newModule,
///      INI::initFromINIMultiProc, \ref sub_objects_modules


/// \class ActivateModuleSpecialPowerModuleData
/// \ingroup grp_w1021_special_power_factories_more
/// \brief Module data for the "ActivateModuleSpecialPower" update module:
///        the special-ability update fields plus a list of
///        \c TriggerSpecialPower entries.
///
/// Built once per module line by
/// ActivateModuleSpecialPowerModuleData::friend_newModuleData and shared
/// by every object of the template. The registration gives
/// ActivateModuleSpecialPower the update interface only.
///
/// \par Modding
/// Fields: the special-ability table of
/// SpecialAbilityUpdateModuleData::buildFieldParse (\c SpecialPowerTemplate,
/// \c StartAbilityRange, \c PreparationTime, \c PackTime, \c UnpackTime,
/// \c EffectDuration and more), then \c TriggerSpecialPower. Each
/// \c TriggerSpecialPower line adds one entry: a name, stored as a name
/// key, and an optional second word, \c TARGETPOS or \c OBJECTPOS
/// (\c OBJECTPOS when omitted or unrecognised). Lines after the eighth
/// entry are ignored without a message. What the name must refer to is not
/// established here.
/// \see ActivateModuleSpecialPowerModuleData::friend_newModuleData

/// \fn ModuleData *ActivateModuleSpecialPowerModuleData::friend_newModuleData(INI *ini)
/// \brief Data factory registered for "ActivateModuleSpecialPower":
///        creates the module data and parses the module's INI block into it.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModuleDataFromINI calls it through the registration.
/// It allocates the data, runs its constructor and, when \p ini is not
/// null, calls INI::initFromINIMultiProc with
/// ActivateModuleSpecialPowerModuleData::buildFieldParse, which chains
/// the special-ability table and the class's own.
///
/// \param ini  The reader positioned on the module's block, or null to
///             keep the constructor defaults.
/// \return The new module data, as the ModuleData base the factory stores.
/// \see ActivateModuleSpecialPowerModuleData


/// \class CombineHordeSpecialPower
/// \ingroup grp_w1021_special_power_factories_more
/// \brief Special power module registered as "CombineHordeSpecialPower",
///        configured by a scan range.
///
/// A SpecialPowerModule subclass with no per-object storage of its own:
/// its instance factory allocates the same size as SpecialPowerModule's.
/// One module per object that lists it, sharing the template's module
/// data.
///
/// \par Modding
/// INI fields of CombineHordeSpecialPowerModuleData: the special-power
/// table (group page), then \c ScanRange (real).
/// \see CombineHordeSpecialPower::friend_newModuleInstance

/// \fn Module *CombineHordeSpecialPower::friend_newModuleInstance(Thing *thing, const ModuleData *moduleData)
/// \brief Instance factory registered for "CombineHordeSpecialPower":
///        creates the module for one object.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModule calls it through the registration when an
/// object whose template lists the module is built. It allocates the
/// module with the global \c operator \c new and runs the constructor.
///
/// \param thing       The object being built; the module keeps it as its
///                    owner.
/// \param moduleData  The template's module data, shared with every other
///                    object of the template.
/// \return The new module, as the Module base the factory stores.
/// \see CombineHordeSpecialPower


/// \class DeflectSpecialPower
/// \ingroup grp_w1021_special_power_factories_more
/// \brief Module registered as "DeflectSpecialPower", with the special
///        power, update and collide interfaces.
///
/// The registration's interface mask is the only one here that includes
/// collide; DeflectSpecialPower::onCollide and DeflectSpecialPower::update
/// are the class's entries in the collide and update interfaces. Its data
/// starts from the short three-field table rather than the special-power
/// table (group page).
///
/// \par Modding
/// INI fields of DeflectSpecialPowerModuleData: only the short table,
/// \c SpecialPowerTemplate, \c InitiateSound and \c StartsPaused. The
/// class adds an empty table of its own, so nothing about the deflection
/// itself is configurable in the module block.
/// \see DeflectSpecialPower::friend_newModuleInstance,
///      DeflectSpecialPowerModuleData::friend_newModuleData

/// \fn Module *DeflectSpecialPower::friend_newModuleInstance(Thing *thing, const ModuleData *moduleData)
/// \brief Instance factory registered for "DeflectSpecialPower": creates
///        the module for one object.
///
/// Same pattern as CombineHordeSpecialPower::friend_newModuleInstance.
///
/// \param thing       The object being built.
/// \param moduleData  The template's DeflectSpecialPowerModuleData.
/// \return The new module, as the Module base the factory stores.
/// \see DeflectSpecialPowerModuleData::friend_newModuleData

/// \class DeflectSpecialPowerModuleData
/// \ingroup grp_w1021_special_power_factories_more
/// \brief Module data for DeflectSpecialPower: the short three-field table
///        and nothing else.
///
/// Built once per module line by
/// DeflectSpecialPowerModuleData::friend_newModuleData and shared by
/// every object of the template. The field list is on DeflectSpecialPower.

/// \fn ModuleData *DeflectSpecialPowerModuleData::friend_newModuleData(INI *ini)
/// \brief Data factory registered for "DeflectSpecialPower": creates the
///        module data and parses the module's INI block into it.
///
/// Same pattern as
/// ActivateModuleSpecialPowerModuleData::friend_newModuleData; the parse
/// procedure is DeflectSpecialPowerModuleData::buildFieldParse (the short
/// table plus an empty one).
///
/// \param ini  The reader positioned on the module's block, or null to
///             keep the constructor defaults.
/// \return The new module data, as the ModuleData base the factory stores.
/// \see DeflectSpecialPower::friend_newModuleInstance


/// \class HordeDispatchSpecialPowerModuleData
/// \ingroup grp_w1021_special_power_factories_more
/// \brief Module data for the "HordeDispatchSpecialPower" special power
///        module: the shared special-power fields and nothing else.
///
/// Built by HordeDispatchSpecialPowerModuleData::friend_newModuleData. In
/// the executable that data factory allocates the same size and runs the
/// same constructor as the data factories of
/// UntamedAllegianceSpecialPower, ManTheWallsSpecialPower and
/// RepairSpecialPower (group page, "Shared factories"), so the four data
/// types share one layout and constructor; each module line still gets
/// its own data object.
///
/// \par Modding
/// A \c HordeDispatchSpecialPower block accepts the special-power table
/// only; the class's own table is empty.
/// \see HordeDispatchSpecialPowerModuleData::friend_newModuleData

/// \fn ModuleData *HordeDispatchSpecialPowerModuleData::friend_newModuleData(INI *ini)
/// \brief Data factory registered for "HordeDispatchSpecialPower":
///        creates the module data and parses the module's INI block into it.
///
/// Builds the data with the constructor it shares with three other data
/// factories (group page) and parses with a procedure that chains
/// SpecialPowerModuleData::buildFieldParse and an empty table.
///
/// \param ini  The reader positioned on the module's block, or null to
///             keep the constructor defaults.
/// \return The new module data, as the ModuleData base the factory stores.
/// \see UntamedAllegianceSpecialPowerModuleData::friend_newModuleData


/// \class ManTheWallsSpecialPower
/// \ingroup grp_w1021_special_power_factories_more
/// \brief Special power module registered as "ManTheWallsSpecialPower",
///        with no settings beyond the shared special-power fields.
///
/// A SpecialPowerModule subclass with no per-object storage of its own
/// (its instance factory allocates the same size as SpecialPowerModule's).
///
/// \par Modding
/// The registered data factory,
/// ManTheWallsSpecialPowerModuleData::friend_newModuleData, parses the
/// special-power table and nothing else. \c SplitHordeSpecialPower uses
/// the same data factory.
/// \see ManTheWallsSpecialPower::friend_newModuleInstance

/// \fn Module *ManTheWallsSpecialPower::friend_newModuleInstance(Thing *thing, const ModuleData *moduleData)
/// \brief Instance factory registered for "ManTheWallsSpecialPower":
///        creates the module for one object.
///
/// Same pattern as CombineHordeSpecialPower::friend_newModuleInstance.
///
/// \param thing       The object being built.
/// \param moduleData  The template's module data.
/// \return The new module, as the Module base the factory stores.
/// \see ManTheWallsSpecialPowerModuleData::friend_newModuleData

/// \class ManTheWallsSpecialPowerModuleData
/// \ingroup grp_w1021_special_power_factories_more
/// \brief Module data built for both ManTheWallsSpecialPower and
///        SplitHordeSpecialPower: the shared special-power fields only.
///
/// Built by ManTheWallsSpecialPowerModuleData::friend_newModuleData, the
/// data factory both registrations name. In the executable that data
/// factory allocates the same size and runs the same constructor as the
/// data factories of UntamedAllegianceSpecialPower, RepairSpecialPower and
/// HordeDispatchSpecialPower (group page, "Shared factories"), so the four
/// data types share one layout and constructor; each module line still
/// gets its own data object.

/// \fn ModuleData *ManTheWallsSpecialPowerModuleData::friend_newModuleData(INI *ini)
/// \brief Data factory registered for both "ManTheWallsSpecialPower" and
///        "SplitHordeSpecialPower".
///
/// Builds the data with the constructor it shares with three other data
/// factories (group page). Its parse procedure is a single jump to
/// SpecialPowerModuleData::buildFieldParse, so the block accepts exactly
/// the special-power table.
///
/// \param ini  The reader positioned on the module's block, or null to
///             keep the constructor defaults.
/// \return The new module data, as the ModuleData base the factory stores.
/// \see ManTheWallsSpecialPower::friend_newModuleInstance,
///      SplitHordeSpecialPower::friend_newModuleInstance


/// \class OCLSpecialPowerModuleData
/// \ingroup grp_w1021_special_power_factories_more
/// \brief Module data for the "OCLSpecialPower" special power module:
///        which object creation list to run and where to create its
///        objects.
///
/// Built by OCLSpecialPowerModuleData::friend_newModuleData and shared by
/// every object of the template. Zero Hour: OCLSpecialPower's targeted
/// use runs its creation list toward the power's target, creating the
/// objects where \c CreateLocation says; the choices include at or above
/// the target, and the map edge nearest the source, nearest the target or
/// farthest from the target. This page does not establish how BFME 2 uses
/// the location names.
///
/// \par Modding
/// Fields, after the special-power table:
/// - \c UpgradeOCL: a science followed by an object creation list; each
///   line adds one pair.
/// - \c OCL: the object creation list.
/// - \c CreateLocation: one of \c CREATE_AT_EDGE_NEAR_SOURCE,
///   \c CREATE_AT_EDGE_NEAR_TARGET,
///   \c CREATE_AT_EDGE_NEAR_TARGET_AND_MOVE_TO_LOCATION,
///   \c CREATE_AT_LOCATION, \c USE_OWNER_OBJECT, \c CREATE_ABOVE_LOCATION,
///   \c CREATE_AT_EDGE_FARTHEST_FROM_TARGET,
///   \c CREATE_CLOSEST_TO_SPAWN_POINT and
///   \c USE_SECONDARY_OBJECT_LOCATION (the whole list in the executable).
/// - \c UpgradeName: an upgrade name; each line adds one.
/// - \c NearestSecondaryObjectFilter: an object filter.
///
/// \note Compared with Zero Hour's table, BFME 2 drops \c ReferenceObject
///       and \c OCLAdjustPositionToPassable and adds \c UpgradeName and
///       \c NearestSecondaryObjectFilter. Its \c CreateLocation list has
///       three more names, one of them inserted third, so a location's
///       stored number differs from Zero Hour's for every name after
///       \c CREATE_AT_EDGE_NEAR_TARGET.
/// \see OCLSpecialPowerModuleData::friend_newModuleData

/// \fn ModuleData *OCLSpecialPowerModuleData::friend_newModuleData(INI *ini)
/// \brief Data factory registered for "OCLSpecialPower": creates the
///        module data and parses the module's INI block into it.
///
/// Same pattern as
/// ActivateModuleSpecialPowerModuleData::friend_newModuleData; the parse
/// procedure is OCLSpecialPowerModuleData::buildFieldParse, which chains
/// SpecialPowerModuleData::buildFieldParse and the class's own table.
/// Zero Hour: the MAKE_STANDARD_MODULE_MACRO_WITH_MODULE_DATA macro in
/// `Common/Module.h` generates the equivalent factory, as a static member
/// of OCLSpecialPower, which constructs OCLSpecialPowerModuleData and
/// parses it with the hand-written
/// OCLSpecialPowerModuleData::buildFieldParse.
///
/// \param ini  The reader positioned on the module's block, or null to
///             keep the constructor defaults.
/// \return The new module data, as the ModuleData base the factory stores.
/// \see OCLSpecialPowerModuleData


/// \class PlayerUpgradeSpecialPower
/// \ingroup grp_w1021_special_power_factories_more
/// \brief Special power module registered as "PlayerUpgradeSpecialPower":
///        when used, gives the controlling player the upgrades its
///        \c UpgradeName lines name.
///
/// A SpecialPowerModule subclass with no per-object storage of its own.
/// PlayerUpgradeSpecialPower::doSpecialPower (the untargeted entry point)
/// does nothing while the object is disabled. Otherwise it first makes a
/// call on its own special-power interface with the value 1.0, which this
/// page does not identify, then looks each listed name up in the upgrade
/// center and hands every upgrade of the player type to the object's
/// controlling player with the completed status, skips upgrades of other
/// types, and finally triggers the power. The player type and completed
/// status are read with Zero Hour's values (\c UPGRADE_TYPE_PLAYER,
/// \c UPGRADE_STATUS_COMPLETE); this page does not identify the Player
/// function that receives them.
///
/// \par Modding
/// INI fields of PlayerUpgradeSpecialPowerModuleData: the special-power
/// table, then \c UpgradeName, which adds one upgrade name per line.
/// List only player upgrades: object upgrades are skipped.
///
/// \warning A name that matches no upgrade stops the loop: the upgrades
///          listed before it are already given, the rest are not, and the
///          power is not triggered.
/// \see PlayerUpgradeSpecialPower::friend_newModuleInstance,
///      PlayerUpgradeSpecialPowerModuleData::friend_newModuleData

/// \fn Module *PlayerUpgradeSpecialPower::friend_newModuleInstance(Thing *thing, const ModuleData *moduleData)
/// \brief Instance factory registered for "PlayerUpgradeSpecialPower":
///        creates the module for one object.
///
/// Same pattern as CombineHordeSpecialPower::friend_newModuleInstance.
///
/// \param thing       The object being built.
/// \param moduleData  The template's PlayerUpgradeSpecialPowerModuleData.
/// \return The new module, as the Module base the factory stores.
/// \see PlayerUpgradeSpecialPowerModuleData::friend_newModuleData

/// \class PlayerUpgradeSpecialPowerModuleData
/// \ingroup grp_w1021_special_power_factories_more
/// \brief Module data for PlayerUpgradeSpecialPower: the shared
///        special-power fields plus a list of upgrade names.
///
/// Built by PlayerUpgradeSpecialPowerModuleData::friend_newModuleData and
/// shared by every object of the template. The field list is on
/// PlayerUpgradeSpecialPower.

/// \fn ModuleData *PlayerUpgradeSpecialPowerModuleData::friend_newModuleData(INI *ini)
/// \brief Data factory registered for "PlayerUpgradeSpecialPower": creates
///        the module data and parses the module's INI block into it.
///
/// Same pattern as
/// ActivateModuleSpecialPowerModuleData::friend_newModuleData; the parse
/// procedure chains SpecialPowerModuleData::buildFieldParse and the
/// \c UpgradeName table.
///
/// \param ini  The reader positioned on the module's block, or null to
///             keep the constructor defaults.
/// \return The new module data, as the ModuleData base the factory stores.
/// \see PlayerUpgradeSpecialPower::friend_newModuleInstance


/// \class RepairSpecialPower
/// \ingroup grp_w1021_special_power_factories_more
/// \brief Special power module registered as "RepairSpecialPower", used
///        on a target object, with no settings beyond the shared
///        special-power fields.
///
/// A SpecialPowerModule subclass with no per-object storage of its own.
/// The class replaces the object-targeted entry point
/// (RepairSpecialPower::doSpecialPowerAtObject). When both the module's
/// own object and the target carry particular kind-of flags, it hands the
/// own object's AI a command aimed at the target, with command source 0
/// (\c CMD_FROM_PLAYER in Zero Hour's enum); otherwise it does nothing.
/// It does not call SpecialPowerModule's version. This page does not
/// identify the command or the two flags.
///
/// \par Modding
/// INI fields: the special-power table only; the class's own table is
/// empty.
/// \see RepairSpecialPower::friend_newModuleInstance,
///      RepairSpecialPowerModuleData::friend_newModuleData

/// \fn Module *RepairSpecialPower::friend_newModuleInstance(Thing *thing, const ModuleData *moduleData)
/// \brief Instance factory registered for "RepairSpecialPower": creates
///        the module for one object.
///
/// Same pattern as CombineHordeSpecialPower::friend_newModuleInstance.
///
/// \param thing       The object being built.
/// \param moduleData  The template's module data.
/// \return The new module, as the Module base the factory stores.
/// \see RepairSpecialPowerModuleData::friend_newModuleData

/// \class RepairSpecialPowerModuleData
/// \ingroup grp_w1021_special_power_factories_more
/// \brief Module data for RepairSpecialPower: the shared special-power
///        fields only.
///
/// Built by RepairSpecialPowerModuleData::friend_newModuleData. In the
/// executable that data factory allocates the same size and runs the same
/// constructor as the data factories of UntamedAllegianceSpecialPower,
/// ManTheWallsSpecialPower and HordeDispatchSpecialPower (group page,
/// "Shared factories"), so the four data types share one layout and
/// constructor; each module line still gets its own data object.

/// \fn ModuleData *RepairSpecialPowerModuleData::friend_newModuleData(INI *ini)
/// \brief Data factory registered for "RepairSpecialPower": creates the
///        module data and parses the module's INI block into it.
///
/// Builds the data with the constructor it shares with three other data
/// factories (group page) and parses with
/// RepairSpecialPowerModuleData::buildFieldParse, which chains
/// SpecialPowerModuleData::buildFieldParse and an empty table.
///
/// \param ini  The reader positioned on the module's block, or null to
///             keep the constructor defaults.
/// \return The new module data, as the ModuleData base the factory stores.
/// \see RepairSpecialPower::friend_newModuleInstance


/// \class ScavengerSpecialPower
/// \ingroup grp_w1021_special_power_factories_more
/// \brief Special power module registered as "ScavengerSpecialPower",
///        configured by a bounty percentage.
///
/// ScavengerSpecialPower::doSpecialPower passes \c BountyPercent to a
/// setter on the object's controlling player, runs
/// SpecialPowerModule::doSpecialPower and then sets a flag the module
/// keeps for itself. This page does not identify the player setter. Zero
/// Hour:
/// CashBountyPower gives a player a bounty percentage through
/// Player::setCashBounty; whether the BFME 2 setter is that function is
/// not established.
///
/// \par Modding
/// INI fields of ScavengerSpecialPowerModuleData: the special-power table,
/// then \c BountyPercent (real).
/// \see ScavengerSpecialPower::friend_newModuleInstance

/// \fn Module *ScavengerSpecialPower::friend_newModuleInstance(Thing *thing, const ModuleData *moduleData)
/// \brief Instance factory registered for "ScavengerSpecialPower":
///        creates the module for one object.
///
/// Same pattern as CombineHordeSpecialPower::friend_newModuleInstance.
///
/// \param thing       The object being built.
/// \param moduleData  The template's ScavengerSpecialPowerModuleData.
/// \return The new module, as the Module base the factory stores.
/// \see ScavengerSpecialPower


/// \class SiegeDeploySpecialPower
/// \ingroup grp_w1021_special_power_factories_more
/// \brief Module registered as "SiegeDeploySpecialPower", with the special
///        power and update interfaces, configured by lower and raise
///        delays, evacuation switches and wall distances.
///
/// Like SiegeDeployHordeSpecialPower it replaces
/// \c initiateIntentToDoSpecialPower
/// (SiegeDeploySpecialPower::initiateIntentToDoSpecialPower). Zero Hour
/// declares that function on SpecialPowerUpdateInterface, the interface
/// of update modules that carry out a special power; it receives the
/// power's template, the target object or position, a waypoint and the
/// command options. Its data starts from the short three-field table
/// (group page).
///
/// \par Modding
/// INI fields of SiegeDeploySpecialPowerModuleData, after
/// \c SpecialPowerTemplate, \c InitiateSound and \c StartsPaused:
/// \c LowerDelay and \c RaiseDelay (durations, written in milliseconds),
/// \c EvacuatePassengersOnDeploy, \c EvacuateCrewOnDeploy and
/// \c SkipAdjustPosition (booleans), and \c WallSearchDistance,
/// \c AwayFromWallWaitDist and \c ExtraWallDistance (reals).
/// \see SiegeDeploySpecialPower::friend_newModuleInstance,
///      SiegeDeploySpecialPowerModuleData::friend_newModuleData,
///      SiegeDeployHordeSpecialPower

/// \fn Module *SiegeDeploySpecialPower::friend_newModuleInstance(Thing *thing, const ModuleData *moduleData)
